diff --git a/docs/configuration.md b/docs/configuration.md
index e8afe5a..2c87397 100644
--- a/docs/configuration.md
+++ b/docs/configuration.md
@@ -841,11 +841,41 @@ kiln memory forget test-runner     # deletes every version of this key
 kiln memory restore 12             # restores this project's version as a new record
 ```
 
+Move memory between projects or machines by exporting one project and importing
+the file into another:
+
+```sh
+kiln memory export > memory.json
+kiln memory import memory.json                         # error on any conflict (default)
+kiln memory import memory.json --dry-run               # validate and report, write nothing
+kiln memory import memory.json --on-conflict skip      # keep existing records
+kiln memory import memory.json --on-conflict replace   # supersede, keeping local history
+```
+
+`import` reads exactly one JSON array in the `export` format and never starts a
+model. It imports into the current project scope, or into the global preferences
+scope with `kiln memory global import <file>`. Flags follow the filename;
+`--on-conflict` accepts `error`, `skip`, or `replace` (default `error`).
+
+The importer never trusts the file's own scope, IDs, timestamps, or lineage. It
+validates every record — active and historical — against the same field, kind,
+and expiry limits as any stored record, then keeps the highest-ID version of
+each key as its current value. A conflict is any existing active key in the
+destination: `error` rejects the whole batch, `skip` leaves it untouched, and
+`replace` supersedes it while preserving local correction history. Project file
+sources are rebound into the current worktree and their SHA-256 revalidated
+against the local files; records whose sources are missing, changed, oversized,
+or escape the project are rejected. Global imports accept source-free
+preferences only. Nothing is written when validation fails, on a dry run, or if
+the transaction cannot complete; on success it prints a one-line JSON summary of
+`inserted`, `replaced`, `skipped`, and `dry_run`.
+
 Explicit user preferences can be shared across projects:
 
 ```sh
 kiln memory global remember response-style "Prefer concise answers"
 kiln memory global export
+kiln memory global import prefs.json
 kiln memory global forget response-style
 kiln memory global restore 12
 ```
